# AUDIOLOOP: audio/Sad_-_Turned_Down.mp3
# CLASS: head1
Mille, Strait and Tall

Mille Barnes steps out of the Port Authority Bus Terminal and heads up to 44th.  The show starts at seven leaving her time to grab a slice before going to work.  Tonight's venue, she pulls out the press info packet,  the "Brickwork Collective".  Brickwork is not a first tier theater, not even second or third.  It is generally the option of last resort for folks either desperate to start/continue careers or groups looking to make political statements.  Generally anything or anyone needing to mention their New York theatrical run.  It provides "a little more than just a stage" to perform on. 
She'd been there before to review some kind of experimental, four-hour-long interpretive dance piece involving live animals and body painting.  Just the kind of venue to host tonight's master work: "The Entropy of Dust". A four-act tragedy.  The press sheet describes it as a "interpretation of self-reflexive avant-garde".  What?   Mille had been warned of the first ninety minutes describing the molecular decay of a single piece of drywall. It is the kind of play that wins awards for "bravery" because no one has actually sat through it twice.
Well, this evening should fill notebooks and generate self-serving conversation for scholastic types.  As long as she can get a solid 500 words to her editor, Keri, by Friday...  The soulless ricotta and marinara slice is handed to her.  A solitary, darkening basil leaf wilts in silence on top.  The vendor hands her the diet coke and she briefly considers emptying the contents of the flask discretely hid in her jacket lining into it.  A habit she inherited from her first mentor on this job.  "Sometimes, it's the only way to push through a particularly unpleasant show," he had said.  She'd adopted his practice and it had honestly come in handy more times than she was willing to admit.   If not during the show, afterward, trying to put a description on paper. No, she was still too professional to start the show under undo influence.

